mentary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265980 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265982 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186266011 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186266011 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-3y-1,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3y-1 \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3y-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-3y-1,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3y-1 \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3y-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186266012 :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=3$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=3,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=3$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=3,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."] {:db/id "8351dfc7-9bd0-5029-9d05-12e3bc502845" :answer/id #uuid "8351dfc7-9bd0-5029-9d05-12e3bc502845" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} [:db/add 17592186262483 :answer-field/choices "8351dfc7-9bd0-5029-9d05-12e3bc502845"] [:db/ensure 17592186243836 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186262122 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186262483 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186265260 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186265450 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186265460 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186265548 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186265629 :answer-field/validate]]
